## Philippine & Southeast Asian Music
@ Kulintang | Angklung | Gamelan | Khaen
Which tradition features a row of small tuned gongs in parts of the southern Philippines?|0|Kulintang is a tuned-gong tradition.
Which Indonesian instrument uses shaken bamboo tubes?|1|Angklung produces pitched bamboo sounds.
Which Indonesian ensemble tradition combines gongs and other instruments?|2|Gamelan is an ensemble tradition.
Which free-reed mouth organ is associated with Lao musical traditions?|3|Khaen uses pipes and free reeds.
@ Oral transmission | Ensemble coordination | Cyclic pattern | Musical context
Learning a piece through listening and demonstration illustrates what?|0|Knowledge can pass through practice without notation.
Players align entries and respond to each other. What skill?|1|Collective timing coordinates the ensemble.
A rhythmic unit returns regularly. What structure?|2|Repetition creates a cycle.
Why examine whether music accompanies ritual, work or celebration?|3|Function affects interpretation.
@ Aerophone | Idiophone | Chordophone | Membranophone
Which classification produces sound mainly from vibrating air?|0|Air is the principal vibrating medium.
Which classification includes instruments whose solid body vibrates?|1|Gongs and bamboo idiophones vibrate as bodies.
Which classification uses vibrating strings?|2|Strings are the sound source.
Which classification uses a stretched vibrating membrane?|3|Drums commonly use membranes.
@ Timbre | Melody | Rhythm | Texture
Different gong sizes produce distinct sound colors. What element?|0|Timbre concerns tone quality.
An ordered sequence of pitches forms what?|1|Melody develops pitch over time.
Patterns of duration and accent form what?|2|Rhythm organizes musical time.
The interaction among simultaneous parts concerns what?|3|Texture concerns the relationship of voices.
@ Avoid a single regional stereotype | Learn performance context | Respect community attribution | Distinguish adaptation from an unchanged tradition
Why should one ensemble not stand for all Southeast Asian music?|0|The region contains diverse traditions.
A ceremonial piece is used in a casual advertisement. What needs examination?|1|Its original function and cultural significance matter.
A recording uses community musicians' work. What ethical concern applies?|2|Credit and appropriate permissions matter.
Traditional instruments are combined with electronic production. What should the description acknowledge?|3|A new arrangement can alter performance practice.
## East Asian Arts
@ Calligraphy | Woodblock printing | Ink painting | Ceramics
Which art emphasizes expressive written brushstrokes?|0|Calligraphy makes writing an artistic practice.
Which process transfers an image from a carved relief block?|1|Raised inked surfaces print the design.
Which art uses variations of ink and brush on a surface?|2|Ink values and marks shape the image.
Which practice forms and fires clay objects?|3|Firing transforms shaped clay.
@ Japan | China | Korea | Cultural exchange
Ukiyo-e woodblock prints developed in which country?|0|Ukiyo-e is a Japanese print tradition.
Chinese calligraphy is centrally associated with which country's artistic history?|1|Its writing and brush traditions have a long history in China.
Hangul is a writing system associated with which country?|2|Hangul is Korean script.
Art forms change as techniques circulate between societies. What process?|3|Exchange can transmit and adapt practices.
@ Line | Value | Negative space | Repetition
A brushstroke changes from thin to thick. Which element is prominent?|0|The line varies with pressure and movement.
Ink washes move from pale to dark. Which element changes?|1|Value concerns lightness and darkness.
Unpainted paper surrounds the subject. Which spatial element is used?|2|The empty areas shape composition.
A decorative motif returns across a surface. Which device?|3|Repeated units form pattern.
@ Technique | Material | Function | Context
Comparing carving and brushwork examines what?|0|Technique concerns how the work is made.
Comparing paper, silk and clay examines what?|1|These are artistic materials.
Comparing a ceremonial object with a household vessel examines what?|2|Their uses differ.
Studying who commissioned a work and when examines what?|3|Patronage and period frame meaning.
@ Do not collapse distinct traditions | Support interpretation with visual detail | Consider translation and provenance | Avoid judging authenticity by age alone
Why should Japanese, Chinese and Korean art not be treated as identical?|0|Their histories and practices differ.
A viewer interprets an unpainted space as silence. What makes the claim defensible?|1|The composition must support the interpretation.
An image's translated label conflicts with its museum record. What should be checked?|2|Reliable records establish identity and wording.
Can a contemporary work use an inherited artistic practice meaningfully?|3|Traditions can continue through adaptation.
## Western Classical Arts
@ Architecture | Sculpture | Painting | Mosaic
Which art organizes built space?|0|Architecture designs structures and environments.
Which art creates three-dimensional forms?|1|Sculpture occupies actual volume.
Which art applies pigment to a surface?|2|Painting builds images through color and marks.
Which technique assembles small pieces into an image?|3|Mosaic combines separate tesserae or pieces.
@ Greek art | Roman art | Renaissance art | Baroque art
Classical idealized human proportions are strongly associated with which ancient tradition?|0|Greek art developed influential proportion systems.
Imperial civic monuments and extensive architectural engineering characterize which ancient tradition?|1|Roman art and architecture served imperial and public purposes.
Renewed engagement with classical antiquity and linear perspective characterize which movement?|2|Renaissance artists developed these interests.
Dramatic movement and strong theatrical effects often characterize which movement?|3|Baroque art frequently emphasizes drama.
@ Proportion | Perspective | Contrast | Patronage
Relationships among parts and the whole concern what?|0|Proportion compares dimensions.
A system makes parallel receding lines meet at a vanishing point. What is used?|1|Linear perspective creates spatial illusion.
Strong light-dark differences emphasize forms. What device?|2|Value contrast directs attention.
A ruler commissions a public monument. Which social relationship matters?|3|Patronage can shape subject and function.
@ Religious function | Political function | Commemorative function | Decorative function
An artwork supports worship and devotion. What role?|0|Its use is religious.
A monument legitimizes a ruler's authority. What role?|1|Its message supports power.
A work marks a remembered event or person. What role?|2|Commemoration preserves public memory.
A repeated motif embellishes a surface. What role?|3|Decoration contributes visual enrichment.
@ Period labels are not universal rules | Representation need not equal endorsement | Material survival affects evidence | Compare works using shared criteria
Why can works from one period differ greatly?|0|Artists and contexts are diverse.
An artwork glorifies conquest. What distinction should analysis maintain?|1|Describing its message is not approving conquest.
Why might surviving stone objects outnumber fragile works in an ancient collection?|2|Preservation conditions shape the record.
How can two monuments be compared fairly?|3|Use relevant criteria such as function, form and context.
## Contemporary Philippine Arts
@ Installation | Performance art | Assemblage | Digital art
A Filipino artist organizes objects and space into a walk-through work. Which form?|0|The environment itself becomes part of the work.
An artist's live action is the primary artistic medium. Which form?|1|Performance uses enacted time and action.
Discarded objects are combined into a three-dimensional artwork. Which form?|2|Assemblage joins existing objects.
An image is created using computational tools. Which form?|3|Digital processes shape the work.
@ Local material | Regional context | Community collaboration | Interdisciplinary work
A work uses bamboo sourced from its community. Which aspect is emphasized?|0|Material links making with local resources.
A mural addresses a locality's fishing livelihoods. Which context matters?|1|Place and experience inform meaning.
Residents help determine a public artwork's design. What process?|2|The community participates in creation.
A project combines dance, projected images and sound. What approach?|3|It crosses artistic disciplines.
@ Concept | Medium | Process | Audience
The artist's central idea is what?|0|The concept guides choices.
Clay, video or bodily movement functions as what?|1|Medium concerns the means of expression.
The stages of making and revising constitute what?|2|Process concerns how a work develops.
The people who encounter and interpret the work are what?|3|Audience participates in reception.
@ Attribution | Permission | Cultural sensitivity | Documentation
Using another artist's image calls for what recognition?|0|Creators should receive credit.
Reproducing restricted material may require what?|1|Legal and ethical authorization can matter.
Using sacred community symbols requires what attention?|2|Their significance should not be trivialized.
Recording an ephemeral work preserves what resource?|3|Documentation supports later understanding.
@ Contemporary does not mean culturally detached | Collaboration needs meaningful participation | Social relevance does not excuse weak evidence | Sustainability includes actual material effects
Why can a new Philippine artwork draw on inherited practices?|0|Present art can engage living traditions.
A project claims community ownership but excludes residents' decisions. What should be examined?|1|Consultation must not be merely symbolic.
A work makes a factual environmental claim. What remains necessary?|2|Artistic purpose does not establish factual accuracy.
A recycled-art project creates toxic waste during production. What assessment is needed?|3|Environmental value depends on the full process.
## Fitness & Active Recreation
@ Aerobic endurance | Muscular endurance | Flexibility | Balance
Which capacity supports sustained walking or cycling?|0|Aerobic systems sustain prolonged activity.
Which capacity supports repeated muscle contractions over time?|1|Muscular endurance resists fatigue in repeated effort.
Which capacity concerns joint range of motion?|2|Flexibility affects available movement range.
Which capacity helps maintain stable body positioning?|3|Balance supports control of posture.
@ Frequency | Intensity | Time | Type
How often a person exercises describes what?|0|Frequency counts occasions.
How hard a session is describes what?|1|Intensity concerns effort.
How long a session lasts describes what?|2|Time concerns duration.
Whether a session uses swimming or dancing describes what?|3|Type identifies the activity.
@ Progression | Specificity | Recovery | Individualization
A plan gradually increases a manageable workload. Which principle?|0|Progression increases demand over time.
Practice is chosen to improve a particular capacity. Which principle?|1|Training effects depend on what is practiced.
A plan allows rest between demanding sessions. Which principle?|2|Recovery permits adaptation.
A plan reflects current ability and health circumstances. Which principle?|3|Individuals require suitable demands.
@ Route planning | Hydration planning | Weather assessment | Equipment check
Before a group hike, identifying access and return points serves what?|0|Route planning helps manage travel.
Preparing suitable water access serves what?|1|Water needs depend on activity and conditions.
Reviewing heat and storm conditions serves what?|2|Weather changes outdoor risks.
Inspecting footwear and activity materials serves what?|3|Sound equipment supports safer participation.
@ Adapt the activity | Review total workload | Prioritize safety over completion | Evaluate progress with comparable measures
A participant cannot safely perform the standard movement. What is appropriate?|0|A suitable alternative supports inclusion.
Fatigue persists as sessions become more demanding. What should be examined?|1|Load and recovery may be mismatched.
Lightning threatens during outdoor recreation. What should guide decisions?|2|A planned goal does not override immediate hazards.
Two fitness scores were taken under different protocols. What is needed before comparing them?|3|Measurement conditions affect interpretation.
## Sports Officiating
@ Impartiality | Rule knowledge | Clear communication | Positioning
Which quality requires decisions without team favoritism?|0|Officials apply standards fairly.
Which competence supports correct application of game provisions?|1|Officials must understand the rules.
Which competence makes decisions and signals understandable?|2|Participants need a clear message.
Which skill helps an official observe relevant play?|3|A useful viewpoint improves observation.
@ Pre-game inspection | Record keeping | Consistent signals | Professional conduct
Checking the playing area before competition serves what?|0|Hazards and readiness should be assessed.
Accurately recording scores and sanctions serves what?|1|Records support correct administration.
Using the prescribed gesture for a decision serves what?|2|Standard signals reduce ambiguity.
Remaining respectful under criticism demonstrates what?|3|Officials should maintain appropriate conduct.
@ Conflict of interest | Error correction | Safety intervention | Rule-based judgment
An official has a substantial personal stake in a team's success. What issue?|0|Private interest may compromise perceived impartiality.
A procedural error is discovered and the rules allow remedy. What process applies?|1|Correction should follow the authorized procedure.
A damaged surface creates an immediate playing hazard. What concern comes first?|2|Participant safety requires attention.
A crowd demands a penalty unsupported by the rules. What should govern?|3|Decisions must follow evidence and applicable rules.
@ Explain the applicable decision | Use the authorized protest procedure | Verify the official record | Consult when permitted
A captain seeks clarification respectfully. What is appropriate?|0|A concise rule-based explanation can help.
A team disputes a decision through a formal mechanism. What should be used?|1|The competition's process governs disputes.
The scoreboard conflicts with the scorer's sheet. What needs checking?|2|A discrepancy requires an authoritative record review.
An official's view was obstructed and consultation is allowed. What helps?|3|Other authorized observations may clarify events.
@ Popularity is not proof of correctness | Consistency includes relevant circumstances | Sport-specific rules must be checked | Admitting a correctable error supports integrity
Why should a loud crowd not determine a ruling?|0|Noise does not establish what occurred.
Why can superficially similar incidents receive different lawful rulings?|1|Relevant rule conditions can differ.
Why should officials not assume every sport uses identical sanctions?|2|Competitions have distinct rule systems.
Why is hiding a known procedural mistake not professionalism?|3|Integrity includes appropriate correction.
## Adolescent Health
@ Puberty | Nutrition | Mental well-being | Social support
What process includes biological changes toward sexual maturation?|0|Puberty involves hormonal and bodily changes.
What supports growth through adequate appropriate food intake?|1|Nutrition supplies energy and nutrients.
What includes emotional functioning and coping?|2|Health includes psychological well-being.
Trusted people who listen and assist provide what?|3|Supportive relationships can help during difficulties.
@ Individual variation | Reliable guidance | Personal boundaries | Help-seeking
Why may classmates experience bodily changes at different times?|0|Developmental timing varies.
What should guide questions about unfamiliar health changes?|1|Qualified information reduces misinformation.
Communicating limits about contact and private information establishes what?|2|Boundaries support autonomy and dignity.
Persistent distress disrupts daily activities. What constructive response is appropriate?|3|Appropriate professional support can help.
@ Peer pressure | Misinformation | Stigma | Protective behavior
A group urges risky behavior to gain acceptance. What influence?|0|Belonging can be used to pressure choices.
An unsourced health claim is repeatedly forwarded. What concern?|1|Repetition does not establish accuracy.
A person is mocked for seeking counseling. What barrier?|2|Stigma can discourage support.
A learner refuses an unsafe challenge. What choice is illustrated?|3|Risk avoidance can protect health.
@ Rest and recovery | Physical activity | Food safety | Trusted communication
A demanding schedule leaves inadequate recovery time. What needs review?|0|Recovery supports functioning.
Regular enjoyable movement supports which health practice?|1|Activity contributes to physical and mental health.
Preventing contamination of meals supports what?|2|Safe handling reduces foodborne risk.
Discussing a concern with a suitable trusted adult supports what?|3|Open communication can connect people with help.
@ Do not equate appearance with health | Consent cannot be assumed | Health advice needs relevant evidence | Privacy does not require ignoring urgent danger
Why is a photograph insufficient to judge someone's full health?|0|Many health conditions are not visible.
Why is silence not automatically agreement to personal contact?|1|Meaningful consent requires appropriate voluntary communication.
A popular influencer recommends a supplement to all teenagers. What should be checked?|2|Safety and suitability are not established by popularity.
A friend reports credible immediate danger. What should be considered?|3|Appropriate protective help can be necessary.
## Lifestyle & Wellness
@ Balanced diet | Regular activity | Recovery | Supportive relationships
A variety of appropriate foods supports what practice?|0|Different foods contribute different nutrients.
Routine walking, dancing or other movement supports what practice?|1|Activity contributes to well-being.
Time for rest between demands supports what?|2|Recovery helps maintain functioning.
Reliable people who provide understanding support what?|3|Social connection can protect well-being.
@ Modifiable behavior | Environmental condition | Health information | Access to services
A person's routine can be adjusted through chosen actions. What factor?|0|Some behaviors are changeable.
Air quality or safe walking space represents what factor?|1|Surroundings affect opportunities and exposure.
Evidence-based guidance represents what resource?|2|Reliable information supports choices.
A nearby affordable clinic represents what resource?|3|Services affect practical care options.
@ Planning | Monitoring | Adjustment | Maintenance
Choosing a feasible activity schedule is what step?|0|Planning specifies intended action.
Recording whether a routine was completed is what step?|1|Monitoring tracks actual behavior.
Changing an unrealistic target after review is what step?|2|Adjustment responds to evidence.
Sustaining a workable routine over time is what step?|3|Maintenance concerns continuity.
@ Serving size | Ingredient information | Relevant professional advice | Personal circumstances
What helps interpret nutrient amounts on a food label?|0|Values refer to a specified portion.
What helps identify components of a packaged product?|1|Ingredients describe its composition.
What is important before using unfamiliar health products?|2|Suitability should be assessed with qualified guidance.
Why may two people need different wellness plans?|3|Needs, resources and conditions differ.
@ Wellness is broader than appearance | Association is not proof of causation | Structural barriers matter | Sustainable change need not be extreme
Why is a lower body weight alone not proof of improved overall well-being?|0|Health includes multiple dimensions.
A habit and an outcome occur together in a survey. What caution applies?|1|Other causes may explain the relationship.
Why should an unsafe neighborhood be considered when advising outdoor exercise?|2|Opportunity is shaped by environment.
Why can a modest feasible routine outperform an unsustainable demanding plan?|3|Long-term adherence matters.
